    {:category :function,
     :zig-version "0.16.0",
     :symbol aguafria.std.Io/async,
     :name "async",
     :signature
     "pub fn async( io: Io, function: anytype, args: std.meta.ArgsTuple(@TypeOf(function)), ) Future(@typeInfo(@TypeOf(function)).@\"fn\".return_type.?)",
     :param-count 2,
     :source "std/Io.zig",
     :zig-name "@import(\"std\").Io.async",
     :documentation
     "Calls function with args, such that the return value of the function is\nnot guaranteed to be available until await is called.\n\nfunction may be called immediately, before async returns. This has\nweaker guarantees than concurrent, making more portable and reusable.\n\nWhen this function returns, it is guaranteed that function has already\nbeen called and completed, or it has successfully been assigned a unit of\nconcurrency.\n\nSee also:\n\nGroup",
     :clojure-name "async",
     :container false,
     :alias-depth 0}
    {:category :function,
     :zig-version "0.16.0",
     :symbol aguafria.std.Io/checkCancel,
     :name "checkCancel",
     :signature "pub fn checkCancel(io: Io) Cancelable!void",
     :param-count 1,
     :source "std/Io.zig",
     :zig-name "@import(\"std\").Io.checkCancel",
     :documentation
     "This function acts as a pure cancelation point (subject to protection; see CancelProtection)\nand does nothing else. In other words, it returns error.Canceled if there is an outstanding\nnon-blocked cancelation request, but otherwise is a no-op.\n\nIt is rarely necessary to call this function. The primary use case is in long-running CPU-bound\ntasks which may need to respond to cancelation before completing. Short tasks, or those which\nperform other Io operations (and hence have other cancelation points), will typically already\nrespond quickly to cancelation requests.\n\nFor a description of cancelation and cancelation points, see Future.cancel.",
     :clojure-name "checkCancel",
     :container false,
     :alias-depth 0}
    {:category :function,
     :zig-version "0.16.0",
     :symbol aguafria.std.Io/concurrent,
     :name "concurrent",
     :signature
     "pub fn concurrent( io: Io, function: anytype, args: std.meta.ArgsTuple(@TypeOf(function)), ) ConcurrentError!Future(@typeInfo(@TypeOf(function)).@\"fn\".return_type.?)",
     :param-count 2,
     :source "std/Io.zig",
     :zig-name "@import(\"std\").Io.concurrent",
     :documentation
     "Calls function with args, such that the return value of the function is\nnot guaranteed to be available until await is called, allowing the caller\nto progress while waiting for any Io operations.\n\nThis has stronger guarantee than async, placing restrictions on what kind\nof Io implementations are supported. By calling async instead, one\nallows, for example, stackful single-threaded blocking I/O.",
     :clojure-name "concurrent",
     :container false,
     :alias-depth 0}
    {:category :global-const,
     :zig-version "0.16.0",
     :symbol aguafria.std.Io/failing,
     :name "failing",
     :signature "std.Io",
     :param-count nil,
     :source "std/Io.zig",
     :zig-name "@import(\"std\").Io.failing",
     :documentation
     "An implementation of Io which simulates a system supporting no Io operations.\n\nThis system has the following properties:\n\nConcurrency is unavailable.\n\nThe stdio handles are pipes whose remote ends are already closed.\n\nThe filesystem is entirely empty, including that the cwd is no longer present.\n\nThe filesystem is full, so attempting to create entries always returns error.NoSpaceLeft.\n\nNo entropy source is supported, so randomSecure always returns error.EntropyUnavailable, and random always returns (fills the buffer) with 0.\n\nNo clocks are supported, so now and sleep always return error.UnsupportedClock.\n\nNo network is connected, so network operations always return error.NetworkDown.",
     :clojure-name "failing",
     :container false,
     :alias-depth 0}
