    {:category :function,
     :zig-version "0.16.0",
     :symbol aguafria.std.Io/futexWait,
     :name "futexWait",
     :signature
     "pub fn futexWait(io: Io, comptime T: type, ptr: *align(@alignOf(u32)) const T, expected: T) Cancelable!void",
     :param-count 4,
     :source "std/Io.zig",
     :zig-name "@import(\"std\").Io.futexWait",
     :documentation
     "Atomically checks if the value at ptr equals expected, and if so, blocks until either:\n\na matching (same ptr argument) futexWake call occurs, or\n\na spurious (\"random\") wakeup occurs.\n\nTypically, futexWake should be called immediately after updating the value at ptr.*, to\nunblock tasks using futexWait to wait for the value to change from what it previously was.\n\nThe caller is responsible for identifying spurious wakeups if necessary, typically by checking\nthe value at ptr.*.\n\nAsserts that T is 4 bytes in length and has a well-defined layout with no padding bits.",
     :clojure-name "futexWait",
     :container false,
     :alias-depth 0}
    {:category :function,
     :zig-version "0.16.0",
     :symbol aguafria.std.Io/futexWaitTimeout,
     :name "futexWaitTimeout",
     :signature
     "pub fn futexWaitTimeout(io: Io, comptime T: type, ptr: *align(@alignOf(u32)) const T, expected: T, timeout: Timeout) Cancelable!void",
     :param-count 5,
     :source "std/Io.zig",
     :zig-name "@import(\"std\").Io.futexWaitTimeout",
     :documentation
     "Same as futexWait, except also unblocks if timeout expires. As with futexWait, spurious\nwakeups are possible. It remains the caller's responsibility to differentiate between these\nthree possible wake-up reasons if necessary.",
     :clojure-name "futexWaitTimeout",
     :container false,
     :alias-depth 0}
    {:category :function,
